<title>NETHERLANDS: Gist, SmithKline settle dispute.</title>
<headline>Gist, SmithKline settle dispute.</headline>
<dateline>DELFT, Netherlands 1997-04-29</dateline>
<text>
<p>Biochemicals firm Gist-Brocades said on Tuesday it had reached an out-of-court settlement with Britain's SmithKline Beecham on the rights to develop and manufacture clavulanic acid.</p>
<p>&quot;As a result of this agreement, Gist-Brocades is free to sell clavulanic acid in markets outside Europe as from now, and in Europe from April 1998 on,&quot; the Dutch group said.</p>
<p>It will not be allowed to sell clavulanic acid, a key ingredient in SmithKline's best selling antibiotic, in countries where SmithKline's patents remain in force. The British group's patent protection in the U.S. remains effective until 2002.</p>
<p>Gist-Brocades said that SmithKline Beecham remained committed to defend its intellectual property rights with regard to clavulanic acid.</p>
<p>But the British firm has acknowledged that Gist-Brocades' current manufacturing process does not infringe SmithKline Beecham's process patents, Gist-Brocades added.</p>
